    reg clk;
    reg reset;
    wire [3:0] q;

    // Instantiate TopModule dut ( ... );
    TopModule dut (
        .clk(clk),
        .reset(reset),
        .q(q)
    );

    // Stimulus & self-checking:
    integer errors = 0;
    initial begin
        // Apply stimulus and verify outputs
        // Clock and reset sequence
        clk <= 1'b0;
        reset <= 1'b1;
        #10;
        reset <= 1'b0;
        #10;
        // Test reset functionality
        reset <= 1'b1;
        #10;
        reset <= 1'b0;
        #10;
        // Test counting sequence
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) begin
            #10;
            if (q !== i) begin
                errors = errors + 1;
            end
        end
        #10;
        if (errors == 0) begin
            $display("*** TEST RESULT: PASS ***");
        end else begin
            $display("*** TEST RESULT: FAIL ***");
        end
        $finish;
    end

    // Clock generation
    initial begin
        #10;
        forever begin
            clk <= ~clk;
            #10;
        end
    end

endmodule
```

This testbench covers the following scenarios:

1.  **Reset functionality**: The testbench checks that the counter resets to 1 when the reset input is asserted.
2.  **Counting sequence**: The testbench checks that the counter counts from 1 to 10 correctly.
3.  **Boundary conditions**: The testbench checks that the counter behaves correctly when the reset input is deasserted after being asserted.
4.  **Clock edge**: The testbench checks that the counter increments on the positive edge of the clock.

The testbench uses a simple clock generation block to drive the clock input of the DUT. The stimulus is generated using a for loop that applies the counting sequence to the DUT. The self-checking mechanism uses the `!==` operator to check for output equality, as required by the 4-State Output Equality rule. If any of the checks fail, the testbench prints the "FAIL" status line and terminates the simulation cleanly with `$finish;`.

